  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nModel trends aren't looking great to cap off August and kick off \nSeptember, as hot and dry conditions are very likely to prevail. \nBoth deterministic and ensemble datasets depict high pressure being \nnudged eastward over the southeast CONUS through the week, as \npersistent troughing settles atop the Pacific northwest. Although \nthis does loosen the grip of ridging over the Panhandles, it should \nstill have enough influence to reduce moisture transport to the CWA \nwhile keeping high temperatures in the 90 to 105 range each day next \nweek. If there are any days with better rain chances, models suggest \nTue-Wed could see slightly improved potential as the jet stream sags \ncloser to the Panhandles. However, this still only supports meager \n10-20% POPs to northwestern portions of the forecast area. Elevated \nfire weather conditions could return any given day with breezy \nwinds, and rain chances don't look promising for awhile looking \nfurther ahead into September. \n\nHarrel",
